    "# 🔮 The Pseudo-Science of Prediction <!-- Heading 1 -->\n",
    "  \n",
    "Humans have always wanted to know the future.\n",
    "  \n",
    "Long before statistical models, machine learning and forecasting software, people used a variety of methods to predict what was yet to happen.  \n",
    "  \n",
    "## 🔮 How did we try to predict the future?\n",
    "  \n",
    "| Method | The idea |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| ♈ **Horoscopes** | The position of celestial bodies reveals future events |\n",
    "| 🔮 **Crystal Ball** | Hidden visions reveal what is to come |\n",
    "| 🍵 **Tea Leaves** | Patterns in tea leaves reveal future events |\n",
    "| 🃏 **Tarot Cards** | Cards provide insight into future outcomes |\n",
    "| 📜 **Prophecy** | Divine or supernatural knowledge reveals the future |\n",
    "| 🐑 **Omen Reading** | Events or physical signs are interpreted as signals of what is to come |\n",
    "   \n",
    "**Observe something today → interpret it → make a statement about tomorrow.**\n",
    "\n",
    "## 📜 A very old question\n",
    "  \n",
    "> *\"Tell us what the future holds, so we may know that you are gods.\"*\n",
    "  \n",
    "— Isaiah 41:23\n",
    "\n",
    "## From Prediction to Forecasting\n",
    "  \n",
    "Today, we still ask exactly the same question.\n",
    "  \n",
    "But modern forecasting asks us to do something very different:\n",
    "  \n",
    "> **Can we use observable evidence and historical data to make predictions  \n",
    "> that are testable, measurable and reproducible? ** \n",
    "  \n",
    "**This is where forecasting begins.**\n",
    "\n",
    "# Learning Objectives\n",
    "  \n",
    ":::{.callout-important title=\"After completing this chapter, you will be able to:\"}\n",
    "  \n",
    "- Explain the concept and purpose of forecasting and distinguish forecasts from goals and plans.\n",
    "    \n",
    "- Identify the key factors that determine forecastability, including relevant drivers, data availability, stability of the past, and forecast interference.\n",
    "  \n",
    "- Describe what constitutes a time series and recognize trend, seasonality, cycles, and irregular variation in time-dependent data.\n",
    "  \n",
    "- Apply a basic Python workflow for data analysis, including data exploration, visualization, correlation analysis, and multiple linear regression.\n",
    "  \n",
    "- Distinguish regression-based analysis from time-series forecasting and explain how the historical behaviour of a variable can provide information about its future.  \n",
    ":::\n",
    "          \n",
    "# What is Forecasting?\n",
    "  \n",
    "> **Forecasting is the process of using information available today to estimate\n",
    "> what is likely to happen in the future.**\n",
    "\n",
    "The information may include:\n",
    "  \n",
    "- Historical data  \n",
    "- Current conditions  \n",
    "- Known future events  \n",
    "- Relevant business information  \n",
    "  \n",
    "::: {.callout-warning title=\"Warning\"}\n",
    "**Forecast ≠ Certainty**\n",
    "\n",
    "A forecast is **not a statement of what will happen**.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is an estimate of **what is likely to happen**, given the information\n",
    "available at the time the forecast is made.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Examples\n",
    "| Forecast              | Decision                               |\n",
    "| --------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n",
    "| Future product demand | How much should we produce?            |\n",
    "| Electricity demand    | How much capacity is required?         |\n",
    "| Customer arrivals     | How many employees are needed?         |\n",
    "| Sales revenue         | How much inventory should we maintain? |\n",
    "| Passenger demand      | How many flights should be scheduled?  |\n",
    "\n",
    "## Myths about Forecasting\n",
    "  \n",
    "> Forecast is **not** a statement of what will happen. It is an estimate of what is likely to happen, given the information available at the time the forecast is made.  \n",
    "   \n",
    "> A Forecast is **not** a plan.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Forecast = what is likely to happen\n",
    "- Goal = what we want to happen  \n",
    "- Planning = actions taken in response to forecasts and goals.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Why Do We Forecast?\n",
    "  \n",
    "The future is uncertain — but decisions cannot always wait for certainty.\n",
    "  \n",
    "Forecasting helps organisations make better decisions by providing an\n",
    "evidence-based estimate of what is likely to happen.\n",
    "  \n",
    "## From uncertainty to action\n",
    "\n",
    "**Future is uncertain**\n",
    "↓\n",
    "**Forecast what is likely to happen**\n",
    "↓\n",
    "**Make a better-informed decision**\n",
    "\n",
    "# Forecasting horizons\n",
    "\n",
    "| Horizon | Typical decisions |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Short term** | Staffing, production, inventory |\n",
    "| **Medium term** | Capacity, procurement, hiring |\n",
    "| **Long term** | Investment and strategic planning |\n",
    "  \n",
    "# What makes a good forecast?\n",
    "   \n",
    "**Not everything is equally predictable**\n",
    "  \n",
    "**Tomorrow's sunrise** → highly predictable\n",
    "  \n",
    "**Tomorrow's lottery numbers** → essentially unpredictable\n",
    "  \n",
    "So the important question is not:\n",
    "  \n",
    "> *\"Can we forecast?\"*\n",
    "  \n",
    "but:\n",
    "  \n",
    "> **\"How forecastable is the thing we are trying to predict?\"**\n",
    "\n",
    "## Four Conditions for Forecastability\n",
    "  \n",
    "1. Understanding of relevant drivers: *Do we understand the important factors associated with the outcome?*\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Data availability: *Do we have sufficient relevant historical information?* \n",
    "  \n",
    "3. Similarity between past and future: *Is it reasonable to expect the future to behave sufficiently like the past?*\n",
    "  \n",
    "4. Forecast non-interference: *Does the forecast itself materially change the behaviour that determines the outcome?*\n",
    "  \n",
    "## Example\n",
    "\n",
    "### Two Contrasting Forecasting Problems\n",
    "  \n",
    "| Dimension | ⚡ Electricity Demand | 💱 Currency Exchange Rate |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **1. Understanding of relevant drivers** | Relatively well understood — temperature, calendar effects and economic conditions are important drivers. | Limited — exchange rates are influenced by many economic, financial and political factors that are difficult to fully model. |\n",
    "| **2. Data availability** | High — several years of demand data and extensive weather data are typically available. | High — extensive historical exchange-rate data are available. |\n",
    "| **3. Similarity between past & future** | Relatively high for short-term forecasts — demand behaviour tends to remain reasonably stable over a few weeks. | Low — financial or political events can cause the future to differ substantially from the past. |\n",
    "| **4. Forecast non-interference** | High — published demand forecasts generally have little effect on residential electricity consumption. | Low — forecasts can influence market participants and therefore affect exchange rates themselves. |\n",
    "| **Overall forecastability** | 🟢 **Relatively high** | 🔴 **Very low** |\n",
    "    \n",
    "# What is a Time Series? \n",
    "    \n",
    "> **A time series is a sequence of observations recorded over time.**\n",
    "  \n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "  \n",
    "- Annual company profits  \n",
    "- Quarterly sales  \n",
    "- Monthly rainfall  \n",
    "- Weekly retail sales  \n",
    "- Daily stock prices  \n",
    "- Hourly electricity demand  \n",
    "\n",
    "**Ordinarily, we are only concerned with the relationship between the two variables. TS takes it one step further by examining the temporal dynamics of this relationship.**\n",
    "  \n",
    "## Time Series vs Cross-Sectional Data\n",
    "\n",
    "| | **Time Series** | **Cross-Sectional** |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Observations** | Same variable over time | Different entities at one point in time |\n",
    "| **Example** | RIL monthly sales, 2015–2025 | Sales of 100 companies in 2025 |\n",
    "| **Primary question** | What happens next? | How do entities differ? |\n",
    "  \n",
    "## Example: Monthly Sales\n",
    "  \n",
    "Suppose a retailer records monthly sales for five years.\n",
    "  \n",
    "| Month | Sales (₹ lakh) |\n",
    "|---|---:|\n",
    "| Jan 2022 | 82 |\n",
    "| Feb 2022 | 79 |\n",
    "| Mar 2022 | 91 |\n",
    "| ... | ... |\n",
    "| Dec 2026 | 156 |\n",
    "  \n",
    "The objective is to use the historical sequence to estimate:\n",
    "  \n",
    "> **What are sales likely to be in January 2027?**\n",
    "  \n",
    "# 🔥 Warming Up with Python: MLR in Action\n",
    "  \n",
    "Before we jump into Time Series, let's warm up with something familiar:\n",
    "  \n",
    "> **Multiple Linear Regression**\n",
    "  \n",
    "We will use Python to move through a familiar analytical workflow:\n",
    "  \n",
    "**Data → Visualize → Correlate → Model → Interpret**\n",
    "  \n",
    "Then we'll ask a different question:\n",
    "  \n",
    "> **What happens when we focus on the behaviour of one variable over time?**  \n",
    "  \n",
    "## Meet the Data\n",
    "  \n",
    "Our dataset contains quarterly observations of cement sales and several variables that may be associated with sales.\n",
    "  \n",
    "| Variable | Description |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Cement Sales** | Quarterly cement sales |\n",
    "| **Advertising** | Advertising expenditure |\n",
    "| **Housing Starts** | New housing construction |\n",
    "| **Cement Price** | Average cement price |\n",
    "| **Rainfall** | Quarterly rainfall |\n",
    "| **GDP Growth** | Quarterly GDP growth |\n",
    "  \n",
    "Our objective is to understand whether these variables contain useful\n",
    "information for explaining and predicting cement sales.\n",
    "\n",
